  <h1>Huỳnh Mai Phúc</h1>
  <p class="kicker">Plone Mraz &middot; Independent researcher &middot; Vietnam</p>

  <h2>Profile</h2>
  <p>
    Self-funded independent researcher working outside any institution. I build
    structural frameworks for how arrangements come to hold, and how the names placed
    over them diverge from what actually holds them up — then carry those frameworks
    into applied domains as bounded, falsifiable proposals. Thirteen preprints and a
    dataset deposited with permanent DOIs, together with two open-source reference
    implementations. My applied work is deliberately cross-domain: I take a structural
    pattern established in one field and test whether it organises observation in
    another, stating the limits of the transfer explicitly rather than leaving them to
    be inferred.
  </p>

  <h2>Professional experience</h2>
  <p>
    Fifteen years in kitchen and manufacturing operations. This is not policy experience
    and is not offered as such. It is the practice context in which I first met the
    problem my research now addresses: the distance between a plan that has been
    approved and the resources actually released to run it. A section chef receives a
    costed menu and must deliver it with the ingredients, equipment and staff genuinely
    on hand; a manufacturing engineering section works the gap between a documented
    process and the line as it runs.
  </p>

