  <h2>Skills and working method</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Observation and cross-domain analysis.</strong> I hold observation,
    description and naming as three simultaneous cuts rather than sequential steps, so
    that what is seen is not quietly replaced by what it is called. On that basis I read
    a structure that recurs across fields sharing no subject matter — clinical
    reasoning, therapeutic response, and now the measurement of governance — and in each
    case state where the transfer stops and the structure would mislead. Evidence is
    taken from published records and traces rather than from what an actor declares
    about itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Technical.</strong> TypeScript — two released implementations. Python and
    Jupyter for formalization work in information theory (Shannon entropy, partial
    information decomposition, effective information). Specification writing: protocol
    documents and machine-readable analytic protocols written to be executed by a reader
    or an agent without inferential gaps. Open research practice: permanent DOI deposit,
    citation metadata, append-only audit design.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Method.</strong> I develop frameworks from direct observation first and
    consult the literature afterwards, recording agreements as convergences rather than
    as sources. I hold apart the mechanism that operates, the fact it produces, and the
    name an observer lays over that fact — and I apply the distinction to my own work,
    which is why earlier formulations in this corpus have been renamed, overturned or
    withdrawn on the public record.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Direction.</strong> The programme is moving from description toward
    measurement: an information-theoretic formalization, with the applied protocol for
    reading which configuration is actually holding, and which is only named as holding,
    now specified and deposited. Environmental and development governance is where I now
    expect that reading to be most useful, and most testable.
  </p>

